---
import Container from './Container.astro'
import { site } from '../../content/site'
import logo from '../../../public/images/logo-ficho.svg?raw'
---
{/* Not sticky — but it DOES have a bar. An earlier version of this comment
    claimed "Figma draws neither"; that was wrong about the bar. Figma's navbar
    (node 8921:2731's parent, 8920:2156) is a 1312x72 pill with a hairline
    border and a fill slightly lighter than the page. It was dropped along with
    the nav links when this header was reduced, and the two were not the same
    decision: the links genuinely are absent from the design, the pill is not.

    Not sticky is still right, and the tests enforce it: a sticky header
    without a background scrolls transparently over the content, which put 88px
    headings underneath the wordmark. With the nav links gone the only thing
    left to pin is the Contact CTA, and that is duplicated in the Contact
    section.

    Why the pill matters beyond fidelity: without it the header has no surface
    of its own and borrows whatever is behind it, so anything painted below —
    the hero's fog, for one — makes it read as a stray dark band.

    Every value here is a token, not a hex. This header also renders on the
    light case-study pages, where BaseLayout remaps the colour tokens; a
    literal would have put a dark bar on #FAFAFA. Measured from Figma at full
    resolution the fill is #232324 -> #1A1B1D and the border is a gradient
    stroke #828287 -> #55555A. The gradient direction is reproduced through
    bg-elevated -> bg-secondary; the border is a flat token rather than a
    gradient ring, which is dimmer than Figma's stroke and the one deliberate
    approximation here. */}
<header class="pt-8">
  <Container>
    <div class="flex h-[72px] items-center justify-between gap-3 rounded-full border border-border-hover bg-[linear-gradient(to_bottom,var(--color-bg-elevated),var(--color-bg-secondary))] pl-8 pr-3">
      {/* Inlined rather than an <img> so the wordmark's currentColor fill can
          inherit --color-text-primary. As an <img> it was Figma's hardcoded
          white, which vanished on the light case-study pages. The accessible
          name lives on the link, so the SVG itself is hidden — naming both
          would make the link announce itself twice. */}
      <a
        href="/"
        class="inline-flex h-8 items-center text-text-primary [&>svg]:h-8 [&>svg]:w-auto"
        aria-label={`${site.name} — home`}
      ><Fragment set:html={logo} /></a>

      <a
        href={`mailto:${site.email}`}
        class="inline-flex h-12 items-center gap-2 rounded-full border border-border px-5 text-base font-semibold leading-none text-text-primary transition duration-300 hover:border-border-hover hover:bg-bg-elevated"
      >
        <span>Contact Us</span>
        <svg
          class="h-6 w-6"
          viewBox="0 0 24 24"
          fill="none"
          stroke="currentColor"
          stroke-width="2"
          stroke-linecap="round"
          stroke-linejoin="round"
          aria-hidden="true"
        >
          <path d="M5 12h14" />
          <path d="m13 5 7 7-7 7" />
        </svg>
      </a>
    </div>
  </Container>
</header>
